<figure class="instant" aria-labelledby="instant-title">
  <figcaption id="instant-title"><span>Figure 1</span> One session, one instant, two clock readings</figcaption>
  <div class="instant-body">
    <p class="fact">Tea tasting · session S501</p>
    <div class="instant-value"><span>Stored instant</span><code>2026-11-04 18:00:00Z</code></div>
    <div class="clock-readings"><div><span>Shown in UTC</span><strong>18:00</strong><small>4 November · offset +00:00</small></div><div><span>Shown in Europe/Stockholm</span><strong>19:00</strong><small>4 November · offset +01:00</small></div></div>
    <p class="separate"><code>display_zone = 'Europe/Stockholm'</code><span>Keep the zone separately when the application needs it.</span></p>
  </div>
  <p class="caption">Both clocks name the same instant. PostgreSQL’s <code>timestamptz</code> preserves that instant, while the zone used on input is not retained as part of the value. The collection’s class-session extension supplies the named display zone.</p>
</figure>
<style>
